@cheese-cakee @hclsys thank you both for the CUDA runs, especially the bf16 tie-group repro with the reference implementation spelled out. That is the case this PR was missing and it is good to see it go to 0 rows on both SM89 and SM_121.

@MarinaMackay I cherry-picked your commit as-is. It is `93f570efc6` on this branch, and cherry-pick kept you as the commit author, so the credit is in the history rather than only in a trailer. Your `Signed-off-by` and your `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5` came through untouched.

On the trailer you asked me to keep: I have not added a separate `Co-authored-by: MarinaMackay <...>` line. This PR's commits use a `Signed-off-by` trailer only, and I do not add `Co-authored-by` trailers. Since the commit lands authored by you, the attribution is already stronger than the trailer would be. If you would rather have the explicit line as well, say so and I will add it on top.

On the change itself, I agree with the analysis, and I checked the guard by hand as well as reading your numbers. When `tl.sum(topk_dup_mask)` is zero the whole first block is a no-op: `tl.cumsum` over an all-zero mask yields zeros, `topk_dup_keep_mask` is `(num_kept_k <= topk_mask_num_keep) & 0` so all false, `num_kept_k` is unchanged, and `keep_mask & (~topk_dup_mask | topk_dup_keep_mask)` is `keep_mask & all_ones`. The `num_keep < num_duplicate_logit` block has the same shape. So a tile without a group member cannot move a bit either way, which matches your 744-call bit-identical sweep.

What I can and cannot confirm here. I have no GPU on this machine, so I re-ran only the CPU side: `tests/v1/sample/test_topk_topp_sampler.py` is 3 passed, 142 skipped on this head, and `ruff check` and `ruff format --check` are clean on `vllm/v1/sample/ops/topk_topp_triton.py`. The 142 skips are the CUDA rows. Your H200 timings and the 744-call equivalence run stay yours; I am quoting them rather than reproducing them, and the PR description says so.

Thanks also for the root cause on the slow mode. The 18-iteration cap leaving `num_keep < num_duplicate_logit`, the midpoint fallback, and the sixth pass falling into the full-vocab duplicate path explains the 45 us jumps I did not have an account for, and the k-sweep numbers are the first end-to-end evidence that this PR removes a slow mode rather than only fixing counts.

